<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sukkur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sukkur is a city on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, opposite Rohri. Nearby lay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aror">Aror</a>, capital of medieval Sindh. The Sukkur Barrage, completed in 1932 under British rule, feeds one of the largest irrigation systems in the world, and the city is an important river crossing and market.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sukkur.jpg" alt="Sukkur" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ibaadnaqvi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rohri.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sukkur stands on the western bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> directly across from the historic city of Rohri, on a small Eocene limestone outcrop that is the most notable land deformation on the vast Indus plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. This outcrop and a similar one on Bukkar Island are sometimes called the Sukkur Gorge, and have traditionally marked the northern boundary of Sindh. The name comes from the Sindhi sakhar, meaning superior. The climate is a hot desert one, and Sukkur was described as the hottest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region has been inhabited for millennia. The ruins of Lakhan-jo-daro, near an industrial park on the outskirts, date from the Mature Harappan period, between 2600 and 1900 BCE, and cover more than 300 hectares. Old Sukkur was a small village until a military garrison was established in 1839. Date palm groves around it were traditionally believed to have grown from the date stones discarded by Arab invaders in the 8th century. Across the river Rohri was a busy port and a major trading centre for agricultural produce by the 1200s, and a minaret 26 metres high was built at Sukkur's shrine of Mir Masum Shah in 1607.</p><p>Modern Sukkur was built under British rule. The garrison was abandoned in 1845, but the town kept growing as a trading centre, and its municipality was constituted in 1862. The Lansdowne Bridge, completed in 1889, was one of the first to cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>. It is made of metal and has two large pylons rather than a series of piers, a cutting-edge design for so wide a span. The Sukkur Barrage, formerly the Lloyd Barrage, was built between 1923 and 1932 under Sir Charlton Harrison to a design by Sir Arnold Musto. It is 1,524 metres long and waters nearly 40,000 square kilometres of farmland through seven canals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Before Partition in 1947 old Sukkur had about 10,000 residents and New Sukkur about 80,000. About 70 per cent of the population was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> in the 1941 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and only 2 per cent in 1951, after most Hindus left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, many of them after riots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a> on 6 January 1948. Muslim refugees from India settled in the city. The Ayub Bridge was built in 1962 beside the Lansdowne Bridge.</p><p>Sukkur is the third largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, with a population of 563,851 in the 2023 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. About three quarters of its people speak Sindhi as a first language. It is a trading and processing centre for the farms of northern Sindh and is known for its dates, and it is the capital of Sukkur Division and Sukkur District.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sukkur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It describes a line of five kings ruling Sindh from Aror, near modern <b>Sukkur</b>, from around the end of the 5th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/rai-dynasty">Rai dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
